{"timestamp":"2026-04-24T18:26:21.115Z","level":"WARN","message":"codex stderr","iteration":5,"chunk":"OpenAI Codex v0.120.0 (research preview)\n--------\nworkdir: c:\\Users\\ben.jones\\Repos\\toolbAX\nmodel: gpt-5.4\nprovider: openai\napproval: never\nsandbox: danger-full-access\nreasoning effort: medium\nreasoning summaries: none\nsession id: 019dc0be-284b-7761-a03f-25da99bb17fe\n--------\nuser\n# Ralph Prompt: continue-progress (cliExec)\n\nA prior Ralph iteration made partial progress. Resume from that durable state and finish the next coherent slice without redoing settled work.\n\nAssume some useful work already landed in the repository. Build on that durable state and avoid redoing completed investigation unless the current files contradict the prior summary.\n\n## Prompt Strategy\n- Target: Codex CLI execution via `codex exec`.\n- Operate autonomously inside the repository. Do not rely on interactive clarification to make forward progress.\n- Keep command usage deterministic and concise because Ralph will persist transcripts, verifier output, and stop signals.\n- End with a compact change summary Ralph can pair with verifier evidence.\n\n## Operating Rules\n- Read AGENTS.md plus the durable Ralph files before making non-trivial changes.\n- Do not invent unsupported IDE APIs or hidden handoff channels.\n- Keep architecture thin, deterministic, and file-backed.\n- Make the smallest coherent change that materially advances the selected Ralph task.\n- Prefer the repository's real validation commands when they exist.\n- For normal CLI task execution, do not edit `.ralph/tasks.json` or `.ralph/progress.md` directly; return the structured completion report instead.\n- Update durable Ralph progress/tasks only when the prompt explicitly targets backlog replenishment.\n\n## Execution Contract\n1. Inspect the workspace facts and selected Ralph task before editing.\n2. Execute only the selected task, or explain deterministically why no safe task is available.\n3. Implement the smallest coherent improvement that advances the task.\n4. Do not edit `.ralph/tasks.json` or `.ralph/progress.md` for normal task execution; Ralph will reconcile selected-task state from your completion report.\n5. Run the selected validation command when available and report the concrete result.\n6. End with a fenced `json` completion report block for the selected task using `selectedTaskId`, `requestedStatus`, optional `progressNote`, optional `blocker`, optional `validationRan`, and optional `needsHumanReview`.\n\n## Final Response Contract\n- Changed files.\n- Validation results.\n- Assumptions or blockers.\n- Known limitations or follow-up work.\n- End with a fenced `json` completion report block for the selected task.\n\n## Template Selection\nThe previous iteration recorded partial progress, so the next prompt should continue from that durable state.\n\n## Preflight Snapshot\n- Ready: yes\n- Summary: Preflight ready: Selected task T2. Validation dotnet test .\\FoToolbox.sln -c Release --filter Category=Testify. Executable token confirmed. Active claims default: T2 - Testify automatic rollback on failure @ 2026-04-24T18:26:18.796Z (fresh). Task graph: ok | Claim graph: ok | Workspace/runtime: 1 info | Codex adapter: 1 warning, 1 info | Validation/verifier: 1 info | Agent Health: 2 info\n- codexAdapter warning: Configured IDE command strategy is unavailable. Missing VS Code commands: claude.openSidebar, claude.newChat. Clipboard handoff can still fall back.\n\n## Objective Snapshot\n# toolbAX — Product Requirements Document\n\n## Overview\n\ntoolbAX is a Windows desktop application for Microsoft Dynamics 365 Finance & Operations (F&O) administrators and developers, inspired by XrmToolBox. It provides a plugin-based host that surfaces operational tools — OData query building, dual-write map browsing, entity inspection, and automated integration validation — against live F&O and Dataverse environments. The application targets .NET 8 Desktop Runtime on Windows 10/11, authenticates via MSAL (Entra ID / service principal), and stores environment profiles locally using SQLite with DPAPI-encrypted secrets.\n\n## Goals\n\n- Deliver a reliable, self-updating tool that reduces manual effort for F&O data engineers and integration specialists.\n[trimmed for size]\n\n## Repo Context\n- Workspace: toolbAX\n- Workspace root: c:\\Users\\ben.jones\\Repos\\toolbAX\n- Inspected root: c:\\Users\\ben.jones\\Repos\\toolbAX\n- Execution root: c:\\Users\\ben.jones\\Repos\\toolbAX\n- Verifier root: c:\\Users\\ben.jones\\Repos\\toolbAX\n- Root selection: Using the workspace root because it already exposes shallow repo markers.\n- Root policy: Inspect, execute, and verify at the workspace root while storing Ralph artifacts under .ralph there.\n- Test roots: tests\n- Validation commands: dotnet test, dotnet test .\\FoToolbox.sln -c Release --no-build --collect:\"XPlat Code Coverage\"\n- Test signals: dotnet test is likely available., README.md may define the canonical build/test commands., Detected test roots: tests.\n\n## Ralph Runtime Context\n- Omitted by prompt budget policy after stable runtime pointers were captured in prompt evidence.\n\n## Task Plan\n- Reasoning: This task closes a data-integrity gap in Testify: once POST succeeds, any later PATCH or CE-verification failure currently risks leaving orphaned FO records and stale cached instance URLs behind. The key challenge is to add rollback only for freshly created records, treat DELETE 404 as a successful no-op during cleanup, and prove the behavior with focused tests around failure paths and persisted configuration state.\n- Approach: Add a best-effort rollback path around the Testify run flow that deletes newly created records on downstream failure, clears persisted instance metadata, and expands cleanup handling/tests to treat already-missing records as success.\n- Steps: Inspect the Testify execution path in `plugins/DualWriteMapBrowser/DualWriteMapBrowserViewModel.Testify.cs` and identify the exact points where a successful POST transitions into PATCH and CE-verification so rollback can be triggered only for records created in the current run. → Add a small helper in the Testify view-model logic to perform rollback DELETE against an entity instance URL, treating HTTP 2xx and HTTP 404 as success, and log rollback outcomes separately from normal cleanup. → Update the main Testify run flow so that if POST succeeded in the current run and any later PATCH or CE-verification step throws, the helper is invoked before the failure is finalized. → Clear `LastEntityInstanceUrl` and `LastRunToken` on the in-memory configuration after a successful rollback, then persist that cleared state through `TestifyConfigurationStore` so stale idempotency data is not reused on the next run. → Preserve existing idempotent reuse behavior by ensuring rollback is skipped for reused records from prior runs; only records freshly created during the current execution should be auto-deleted on failure.\n- Risks: Rollback could accidentally delete a reused record from a prior run if the implementation does not clearly track whether the current execution created the entity.; Clearing configuration state only in memory without persisting it would leave `LastEntityInstanceUrl` stale across app restarts.; DELETE 404 handling must be limited to rollback/cleanup semantics; broadening it carelessly could hide genuine failures in other write paths.\n- Suggested validation: dotnet test tests/FoToolbox.Tests/FoToolbox.Tests.csproj --filter \"FullyQualifiedName~Testify\"\n\n## Task Focus\n- Backlog counts: todo 4, in_progress 1, blocked 0, done 1\n- Next actionable task: T2 (in_progress)\n- Selected task id: T2\n- Title: Testify automatic rollback on failure\n- Status: in_progress\n- Parent task: none\n- Dependencies: T1 (done)\n- Direct children: none\n- Remaining descendants: none\n- Task validation hint: dotnet test .\\FoToolbox.sln -c Release --filter Category=Testify\n- Effective validation command: dotnet test .\\FoToolbox.sln -c Release --filter Category=Testify\n- Validation command normalized from: none\n- Notes: none\n- Blocker: none\n- Acceptance criteria: (1) If PATCH or CE-verification fails after a successful POST, the created record is deleted automatically (2) lastEntityInstanceUrl is cleared from TestifyConfigurationStore after rollback (3) Cleanup phase returns success (no error) when a cached URL references a record already deleted externally\n- Constraints: none\n- Relevant files: plugins/DualWriteMapBrowser/TestifyRunner.cs, plugins/DualWriteMapBrowser/DualWriteMapBrowserViewModel.Testify.cs, plugins/DualWriteMapBrowser/TestifyConfigurationStore.cs\n- Task-local code context: plugins/DualWriteMapBrowser/TestifyRunner.cs, plugins/DualWriteMapBrowser/DualWriteMapBrowserViewModel.Testify.cs, plugins/DualWriteMapBrowser/TestifyConfigurationStore.cs\n\n## Recent Progress\n- Ralph workspace initialized.\n- Use this file for durable progress notes between fresh Codex runs.\n- Added a testable TestifyConfigurationStore injection seam for DualWriteMapBrowserViewModel, removed ConfigureAwait(false) from the WPF Testify settings load/save path, and added a regression test covering per-map settings load, map switching, save, and reload across a fresh view-model instance. Release\n[trimmed for size]\n\n## Prior Iteration Evidence\n- Prior iteration: 4\n- Prior outcome classification: partial_progress\n- Prior execution / verification: succeeded / failed\n- Prior validation failure signature: dotnet test .\\FoToolbox.sln -c Release --filter Category=Testify::exit:1::Testhost process for source(s) 'c:\\Users\\ben.jones\\Repos\\toolbAX\\tests\\FoToolbox.Tests\\bin\\Release\\net8.0-windows\\FoToolbox.Tests.dll' exited with error: You must install or update .NET to run this application. | App: c:\\Users\\ben.jones\\Rep\n- Additional prior-context signals omitted: 6.\n\n"}
