ent the smallest coherent improvement that advances the task.\n4. Do not edit `.ralph/tasks.json` or `.ralph/progress.md` for normal task execution; Ralph will reconcile selected-task state from your completion report.\n5. Run the selected validation command when available and report the concrete result.\n6. End with a fenced `json` completion report block for the selected task using `selectedTaskId`, `requestedStatus`, optional `progressNote`, optional `blocker`, optional `validationRan`, and optional `needsHumanReview`.\n\n## Final Response Contract\n- Changed files.\n- Validation results.\n- Assumptions or blockers.\n- Known limitations or follow-up work.\n- End with a fenced `json` completion report block for the selected task.\n\n## Template Selection\nThe previous iteration recorded partial progress, so the next prompt should continue from that durable state.\n\n## Preflight Snapshot\n- Ready: yes\n- Summary: Preflight ready: Selected task T2. Validation dotnet test .\\FoToolbox.sln -c Release --filter Category=Testify. Executable token confirmed. Active claims default: T2 - Testify automatic rollback on failure @ 2026-04-24T18:40:48.684Z (fresh). Task graph: ok | Claim graph: ok | Workspace/runtime: 1 info | Codex adapter: 1 warning, 1 info | Validation/verifier: 1 info | Agent Health: 2 info\n- codexAdapter warning: Configured IDE command strategy is unavailable. Missing VS Code commands: claude.openSidebar, claude.newChat. Clipboard handoff can still fall back.\n\n## Objective Snapshot\n# toolbAX — Product Requirements Document\n\n## Overview\n\ntoolbAX is a Windows desktop application for Microsoft Dynamics 365 Finance & Operations (F&O) administrators and developers, inspired by XrmToolBox. It provides a plugin-based host that surfaces operational tools — OData query building, dual-write map browsing, entity inspection, and automated integration validation — against live F&O and Dataverse environments. The application targets .NET 8 Desktop Runtime on Windows 10/11, authenticates via MSAL (Entra ID / service principal), and stores environment profiles locally using SQLite with DPAPI-encrypted secrets.\n\n## Goals\n\n- Deliver a reliable, self-updating tool that reduces manual effort for F&O data engineers and integration specialists.\n[trimmed for size]\n\n## Repo Context\n- Omitted by prompt budget policy after core root and task context were captured in prompt evidence.\n\n## Ralph Runtime Context\n- Omitted by prompt budget policy after stable runtime pointers were captured in prompt evidence.\n\n## Task Plan\n- Reasoning: This task closes a data-integrity gap in Testify: once POST succeeds, any later PATCH or CE-verification failure currently risks leaving orphaned FO records and stale cached instance URLs behind. The key challenge is to add rollback only for freshly created records, treat DELETE 404 as a successful no-op during cleanup, and prove the behavior with focused tests around failure paths and persisted configuration state.\n- Approach: Add a best-effort rollback path around the Testify run flow that deletes newly created records on downstream failure, clears persisted instance metadata, and expands cleanup handling/tests to treat already-missing records as success.\n- Steps: Inspect the Testify execution path in `plugins/DualWriteMapBrowser/DualWriteMapBrowserViewModel.Testify.cs` and identify the exact points where a successful POST transitions into PATCH and CE-verification so rollback can be triggered only for records created in the current run. → Add a small helper in the Testify view-model logic to perform rollback DELETE against an entity instance URL, treating HTTP 2xx and HTTP 404 as success, and log rollback outcomes separately from normal cleanup. → Update the main Testify run flow so that if POST succeeded in the current run and any later PATCH or CE-verification step throws, the helper is invoked before the failure is finalized. → Clear `LastEntityInstanceUrl` and `LastRunToken` on the in-memory configuration after a successful rollback, then persist that cleared state through `TestifyConfigurationStore` so stale idempotency data is not reused on the next run. → Preserve existing idempotent reuse behavior by ensuring rollback is skipped for reused records from prior runs; only records freshly created during the current execution should be auto-deleted on failure.\n- Risks: Rollback could accidentally delete a reused record from a prior run if the implementation does not clearly track whether the current execution created the entity.; Clearing configuration state only in memory without persisting it would leave `LastEntityInstanceUrl` stale across app restarts.; DELETE 404 handling must be limited to rollback/cleanup semantics; broadening it carelessly could hide genuine failures in other write paths.\n- Suggested validation: dotnet test tests/FoToolbox.Tests/FoToolbox.Tests.csproj --filter \"FullyQualifiedName~Testify\"\n\n## Task Focus\n- Backlog counts: todo 4, in_progress 1, blocked 0, done 1\n- Next actionable task: T2 (in_progress)\n- Selected task id: T2\n- Title: Testify automatic rollback on failure\n- Status: in_progress\n- Parent task: none\n- Dependencies: T1 (done)\n- Direct children: none\n- Remaining descendants: none\n- Task validation hint: dotnet test .\\FoToolbox.sln -c Release --filter Category=Testify\n- Effective validation command: dotnet test .\\FoToolbox.sln -c Release --filter Category=Testify\n- Validation command normalized from: none\n- Notes: Extracted FinalizeTestifyFailureAsync so rollback remains limited to records created in the current run, and added regression tests covering fresh-record rollback state clearing plus reused-record rollback skipping. Focused test project build passes.\n- Blocker: dotnet test .\\FoToolbox.sln -c Release --filter Category=Testify fails on this machine because x64 Microsoft.AspNetCore.App 8.0 is not installed for testhost.exe.\n- Acceptance criteria: (1) If PATCH or CE-verification fails